        <!-- ===================================================== -->
    <!-- Stem -->
    <!-- ===================================================== -->
    
    <section class="mb-12 rounded-2xl border bg-white p-8">
    
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
    
        <div>
    
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold">
            3. Stem
          </h3>
    
          <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600">
            The core segment of a word.
          </p>
    
        </div>
    
        <a href="/cms/morphology_word_segments?q%5Bsegment_type_eq%5D=Stem" class="btn btn-sm btn-info text-white">
          Browse Stems
        </a>
    
      </div>
    
      <p class="mt-6 leading-8">
        The stem is what remains of a written word once every prefix and suffix
        is stripped away. Every word has exactly one stem; prefixes and suffixes
        are optional. The stem is the segment that actually carries the root and
        pattern, and it is the piece the rest of the analysis (root, lemma,
        part of speech, case) attaches to.
      </p>
    
      <div class="mt-6 rounded-lg bg-slate-50 p-5">
    
        <div class="font-semibold mb-3">Stem vs Root vs Lemma</div>
    
        <table class="min-w-full">
          <tbody class="divide-y">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold w-32">Stem</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 text-xl">سْمِ</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-600">The core segment as written in بِسْمِ, inflected for its position (genitive).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Lemma</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 text-xl">ٱسْم</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-600">The dictionary form that stem points back to.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Root</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 text-xl">س م و</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-600">The abstract consonant skeleton behind the lemma.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
    
      </div>
    
      <p class="mt-6 text-slate-600 leading-8">
        In both datasets segments are typed as Prefix, Stem or Suffix:
        <a href="/cms/morphology_word_segments?q%5Bsegment_type_eq%5D=Stem" class="text-blue-600">Word Segment stems →</a>
        <a href="/cms/morphology_word_tokens?q%5Bsegment_type_eq%5D=Stem" class="text-blue-600 ml-3">NoorBayan token stems →</a>
      </p>
    
    </section>
    

