    <section class="mb-8">

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">
        Grammar (Syntax)
      </h2>

      <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 max-w-4xl">

        If morphology studies an individual word, grammar studies
        <strong>how words work together</strong> to form a complete sentence.

      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-8">

        <div class="rounded-xl border bg-white p-8">

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold">
            Morphology asks...
          </h3>

          <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 list-disc ml-6">

            <li>What is this word?</li>

            <li>What is its root?</li>

            <li>What is its lemma?</li>

            <li>Is it a noun or a verb?</li>

            <li>What prefixes and suffixes does it contain?</li>

          </ul>

        </div>

        <div class="rounded-xl border bg-white p-8">

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold">
            Grammar asks...
          </h3>

          <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 list-disc ml-6">

            <li>Who is the subject?</li>

            <li>What is the object?</li>

            <li>Which words modify other words?</li>

            <li>How does this sentence fit together?</li>

          </ul>

        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="mt-8 rounded-xl border border-blue-200 bg-blue-50 p-6">

        <h3 class="font-semibold text-blue-900">
          Think of it this way
        </h3>

        <p class="mt-3 text-blue-800 leading-8">

          Morphology is about the
          <strong>inside of a word.</strong>

          Grammar is about the
          <strong>relationships between words.</strong>

        </p>

      </div>

    </section>

